## 6. Pros and Cons

### Pros
| Area | Benefit |
|---|---|
| **Latency** | Near real-time (seconds to 1 minute end-to-end) |
| **No source impact** | Debezium reads binlog — zero load on MySQL queries |
| **Full audit trail** | Bronze preserves every CDC event forever |
| **Exactly-once** | DLT APPLY CHANGES handles duplicate events via sequence_by |
| **Schema evolution** | DLT + Delta handle MySQL DDL changes gracefully |
| **Scalability** | Kafka decouples source from sink — spike absorption |
| **Replay** | Kafka retention allows re-reading historical events for recovery |
| **Operational simplicity** | DLT manages checkpoints, retries, schema automatically |
| **DELETE semantics** | Debezium captures hard deletes — rare in traditional ETL |
| **GDPR compliance** | Hard deletes in silver + bronze soft-delete flag |

### Cons
| Area | Limitation |
|---|---|
| **Operational complexity** | 4 separate systems to maintain (MySQL, Debezium, Kafka, Databricks) |
| **Initial snapshot cost** | Full table snapshot locks MySQL briefly; large tables take hours |
| **Schema coupling** | Debezium schema registry must stay in sync with MySQL schema |
| **Binlog retention** | If Debezium connector is down > binlog retention period → full re-snapshot needed |
| **Out-of-order events** | Kafka partition ordering guarantees not global — sequence_by is essential |
| **DELETE complexity** | Hard deletes require careful handling to avoid losing data in bronze |
| **Cost** | Kafka cluster + Debezium Connect cluster + Databricks compute = significant cost |
| **Latency floor** | Micro-batch mode = 30-60 second latency; continuous mode is expensive |
| **Debugging difficulty** | Tracing a single row through 4 systems is non-trivial |

